.SH ::RBC
.SH COMMANDS
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIbind\fP
Queries or modifies bindings associated with axis binding tags\&. With no arguments, returns the currently registered axis binding tags\&. With a tag name, returns the event sequences bound to that tag\&. With a sequence, returns its binding script\&. With a command, creates or replaces the binding\&.
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of an axis configuration option\&. See \fIaxis options\fP for the complete option reference\&. Current value of option\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies axis configuration\&. When querying, normally one axis name is supplied\&. When setting options, multiple axis names may precede the option/value pairs and the same configuration is applied to each named axis\&. See \fIaxis options\fP for the complete option reference\&.
.TP
\fIcreate\fP
Creates a new virtual axis\&. The new axis is not displayed in a margin until it is assigned with a margin-axis use operation, although elements may be mapped to it while it remains virtual\&. Axis names must be unique within the graph and may not begin with -\&.
.TP
\fIdelete\fP
Deletes one or more virtual axes\&. If an axis is still referenced by an element, physical destruction is deferred until the reference is released, but the deleted name immediately stops referring to that axis and may be reused for a newly created axis\&.
.TP
\fIget\fP
Returns information about the axis currently selected by the graph binding machinery\&. current returns the axis name\&. detail returns label, title, or line depending on the part of the axis under the current binding position\&. If no axis is current, the result is empty\&. Current axis name, current axis detail, or an empty result\&.
.TP
\fIinvtransform\fP
Converts an integer window coordinate to the corresponding graph value using the specified axis\&. The axis orientation and -descending setting are taken into account\&. Logarithmic axes return the corresponding positive data value\&.
.TP
\fIlimits\fP
Returns the effective displayed minimum and maximum of the specified axis\&. Automatic scaling, explicit limits, loose-range processing, and logarithmic mapping are reflected in the returned values\&. Logarithmic limits are returned as ordinary positive data values rather than base-10 exponents\&. Two-element list min max\&.
.TP
\fInames\fP
Returns the names of existing axes\&. If one or more Tcl glob patterns are supplied, an axis is returned when its name matches any pattern\&. Axes pending deletion are not returned\&. List of matching axis names\&.
.TP
\fItransform\fP
Converts a graph value to an integer window coordinate using the specified axis\&. value is evaluated as a Tcl numeric expression and must be finite\&. Values transformed through a logarithmic axis must be positive\&.
.TP
\fIview\fP
Queries or changes the visible portion of an axis scrolling region\&. With no additional arguments, returns normalized first last fractions\&. moveto selects an absolute fraction\&. scroll moves by units or pages; a page is 90 percent of the current viewport\&. A single integer is accepted as a compatibility shorthand for scrolling that number of units\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS bind\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies bindings associated with axis binding tags\&. With no arguments, returns the currently
registered axis binding tags\&. With a tag name, returns the event sequences bound to that tag\&. With a
sequence, returns its binding script\&. With a command, creates or replaces the binding\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS bind\fP \fItagName\fP
.br
\fBAXIS bind\fP \fItagName sequence\fP
.br
\fBAXIS bind\fP \fItagName sequence command\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
command
Tcl script to execute for the binding\&.
.TP
sequence
Tk event sequence\&.
.TP
tagName
Axis name, axis class, or user-defined axis binding tag\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Binding tags, event sequences, or a binding script when querying; nothing when setting a binding\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of an axis configuration option\&. See \fIaxis options\fP for the complete
option reference\&.
Current value of option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS cget\fP \fIaxisName option\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
axisName
Name of the axis\&.
.TP
option
Axis configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of an axis configuration option\&. See \fIaxis options\fP for the complete
option reference\&.
Current value of option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies axis configuration\&. When querying, normally one axis name is supplied\&. When setting
options, multiple axis names may precede the option/value pairs and the same configuration is applied to
each named axis\&. See \fIaxis options\fP for the complete option reference\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS configure\fP \fIaxisName\fP
.br
\fBAXIS configure\fP \fIaxisName option\fP
.br
\fBAXIS configure\fP \fIaxisName ?axisName \&.\&.\&.? ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
axisName
Name of an axis to query or configure\&.
.TP
option
Axis configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying; nothing when setting options\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS create\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a new virtual axis\&. The new axis is not displayed in a margin until it is assigned with a
margin-axis use operation, although elements may be mapped to it while it remains virtual\&. Axis names must
be unique within the graph and may not begin with -\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS create\fP \fIaxisName ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
axisName
Name of the new axis\&.
.TP
option
Axis configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Initial value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Name of the newly created axis\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS delete\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Deletes one or more virtual axes\&. If an axis is still referenced by an element, physical destruction is
deferred until the reference is released, but the deleted name immediately stops referring to that axis and
may be reused for a newly created axis\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS delete\fP \fI?axisName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
axisName
Name of an axis to delete\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS get\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns information about the axis currently selected by the graph binding machinery\&. current returns the
axis name\&. detail returns label, title, or line depending on the part of the axis under the current
binding position\&. If no axis is current, the result is empty\&.
Current axis name, current axis detail, or an empty result\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS get\fP \fIname\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
name
Information to retrieve; must be current or detail\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns information about the axis currently selected by the graph binding machinery\&. current returns the
axis name\&. detail returns label, title, or line depending on the part of the axis under the current
binding position\&. If no axis is current, the result is empty\&.
Current axis name, current axis detail, or an empty result\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS invtransform\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Converts an integer window coordinate to the corresponding graph value using the specified axis\&. The axis
orientation and -descending setting are taken into account\&. Logarithmic axes return the corresponding
positive data value\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS invtransform\fP \fIaxisName value\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
axisName
Name of the axis used for the conversion\&.
.TP
value
Integer window coordinate\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Corresponding graph-coordinate value\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS limits\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the effective displayed minimum and maximum of the specified axis\&. Automatic scaling, explicit
limits, loose-range processing, and logarithmic mapping are reflected in the returned values\&. Logarithmic
limits are returned as ordinary positive data values rather than base-10 exponents\&.
Two-element list min max\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS limits\fP \fIaxisName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
axisName
Name of the axis\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the effective displayed minimum and maximum of the specified axis\&. Automatic scaling, explicit
limits, loose-range processing, and logarithmic mapping are reflected in the returned values\&. Logarithmic
limits are returned as ordinary positive data values rather than base-10 exponents\&.
Two-element list min max\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS names\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the names of existing axes\&. If one or more Tcl glob patterns are supplied, an axis is returned when
its name matches any pattern\&. Axes pending deletion are not returned\&.
List of matching axis names\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS names\fP \fI?pattern \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pattern
Optional Tcl glob pattern used to select axis names\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the names of existing axes\&. If one or more Tcl glob patterns are supplied, an axis is returned when
its name matches any pattern\&. Axes pending deletion are not returned\&.
List of matching axis names\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS transform\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Converts a graph value to an integer window coordinate using the specified axis\&. value is evaluated as a
Tcl numeric expression and must be finite\&. Values transformed through a logarithmic axis must be positive\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS transform\fP \fIaxisName value\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
axisName
Name of the axis used for the conversion\&.
.TP
value
Graph-coordinate value or Tcl numeric expression\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Corresponding integer window coordinate\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBAXIS view\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes the visible portion of an axis scrolling region\&. With no additional arguments, returns
normalized first last fractions\&. moveto selects an absolute fraction\&. scroll moves by units or pages;
a page is 90 percent of the current viewport\&. A single integer is accepted as a compatibility shorthand for
scrolling that number of units\&.
.RS 4n
\fBAXIS view\fP \fIaxisName\fP
.br
\fBAXIS view\fP \fIaxisName moveto fraction\fP
.br
\fBAXIS view\fP \fIaxisName scroll number units\fP
.br
\fBAXIS view\fP \fIaxisName scroll number pages\fP
.br
\fBAXIS view\fP \fIaxisName number\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
axisName
Name of the axis\&.
.TP
fraction
Normalized position used by moveto\&.
.TP
number
Signed number of units or pages to scroll\&.
.TP
what
Scrolling unit; must be units or pages\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Two-element list first last when querying; nothing when changing the view\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBbarchart\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a barchart widget\&. A barchart uses bar elements as its default element type\&. It uses the same graph
widget infrastructure and common components as graph, but elements created through the generic
GRAPHINST element interface are bar elements by default\&. Configuration options supplied when the widget
is created are the same options accepted later by GRAPHINST configure\&.
.RS 4n
\fBbarchart\fP \fIpathName ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pathName
Tk pathname of the barchart widget to create\&.
.TP
option
Graph widget configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Value for the configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
barchart \&.b -title "Example barchart" -width 800 -height 500
pack \&.b -fill both -expand yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The pathname of the newly created barchart widget\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of one crosshairs configuration option\&. See \fICrosshairs options\fP\&. Current value of the requested option\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies crosshairs configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns all configuration information\&. With one option, returns information for that option\&. Otherwise args contains option/value pairs to change\&. See \fICrosshairs options\fP\&.
.TP
\fIoff\fP
Hides the crosshairs\&. This is equivalent to setting -hide yes\&. If the crosshairs are already hidden, the operation has no effect\&.
.TP
\fIon\fP
Enables the crosshairs by setting -hide no\&. The lines are drawn only when the configured position lies inside the plotting area; an empty or out-of-range position may therefore leave enabled crosshairs invisible\&.
.TP
\fItoggle\fP
Toggles crosshair visibility\&. Enabled crosshairs become hidden and hidden crosshairs become enabled\&. The resulting state is reflected by the -hide configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of one crosshairs configuration option\&. See
\fICrosshairs options\fP\&.
Current value of the requested option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS cget\fP \fIoption\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Crosshairs configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of one crosshairs configuration option\&. See
\fICrosshairs options\fP\&.
Current value of the requested option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies crosshairs configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns all configuration
information\&. With one option, returns information for that option\&. Otherwise args contains option/value
pairs to change\&. See \fICrosshairs options\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS configure\fP \fI?option? ?value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Crosshairs configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying, otherwise nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS off\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Hides the crosshairs\&. This is equivalent to setting -hide yes\&. If the crosshairs are already hidden, the
operation has no effect\&.
.RS 4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS off\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS on\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Enables the crosshairs by setting -hide no\&. The lines are drawn only when the configured position lies
inside the plotting area; an empty or out-of-range position may therefore leave enabled crosshairs invisible\&.
.RS 4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS on\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS toggle\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Toggles crosshair visibility\&. Enabled crosshairs become hidden and hidden crosshairs become enabled\&. The
resulting state is reflected by the -hide configuration option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBCROSSHAIRS toggle\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIactivate\fP
Queries or changes the active state of graph elements\&. With no arguments, returns the names of all active elements\&. With an element name and no indices, activates the complete element\&. With one or more indices, activates only those data points and replaces the element's previous active-index selection\&.
.TP
\fIbind\fP
Queries or modifies event bindings for element binding tags\&. With no arguments, returns the currently known element binding tags\&. With a tag name, returns the event sequences bound to that tag\&. With a tag and sequence, returns the corresponding binding script\&. With a command, creates or replaces the binding\&.
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of an element configuration option\&. The available options depend on the concrete element type\&. Current value of option\&.
.TP
\fIclosest\fP
Finds the visible element nearest to the specified window coordinate\&. On success, stores information in the Tcl array varName using the keys name, index, x, y, and dist\&. Searches all visible elements unless one or more element names are supplied after the options\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies element configuration\&. When querying, one element name is supplied\&. When setting options, multiple element names may precede the option/value pairs and the same configuration is applied to each named element\&.
.TP
\fIcreate\fP
Creates a new graph element\&. GRAPHINST element create uses the widget's default element type:
.TP
\fIdeactivate\fP
Clears the active state and any active-point selection for one or more elements\&.
.TP
\fIdelete\fP
Deletes one or more elements and releases their associated resources, bindings, legend entries, vector references, and display-list entries\&.
.TP
\fIexists\fP
Tests whether an element with the specified name exists in the graph\&.
.TP
\fIget\fP
Returns the element currently selected by the graph binding machinery\&. The only supported selector is current\&. If there is no current element, the result is empty\&. Current element name or an empty result\&.
.TP
\fInames\fP
Returns the names of existing graph elements\&. With no patterns, all element names are returned\&. With one or more Tcl glob patterns, an element is returned if its name matches any pattern\&. List of matching element names\&.
.TP
\fIshow\fP
Queries or replaces the element display list\&. With no argument, returns the currently displayed elements in drawing order\&. With elemList, displays only the listed elements and hides all others\&. The list order determines drawing order; later elements are drawn above earlier elements\&. Unknown element names are ignored\&. An empty list hides all elements\&.
.TP
\fItype\fP
Returns the concrete class of the named element\&. Current results are LineElement, BarElement, and StripElement\&. Element class name\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT activate\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes the active state of graph elements\&. With no arguments, returns the names of all active
elements\&. With an element name and no indices, activates the complete element\&. With one or more indices,
activates only those data points and replaces the element's previous active-index selection\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT activate\fP \fIelemName ?index \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
elemName
Name of the element to activate\&.
.TP
index
Data index to activate; may be end or a Tcl integer expression\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
List of active element names when querying; nothing when changing activation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT bind\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies event bindings for element binding tags\&. With no arguments, returns the currently known
element binding tags\&. With a tag name, returns the event sequences bound to that tag\&. With a tag and
sequence, returns the corresponding binding script\&. With a command, creates or replaces the binding\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT bind\fP \fItagName\fP
.br
\fBELEMENT bind\fP \fItagName sequence\fP
.br
\fBELEMENT bind\fP \fItagName sequence command\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
command
Tcl script to execute for the binding\&.
.TP
sequence
Tk event sequence\&.
.TP
tagName
Element name, element class, or user-defined binding tag\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Binding tags, event sequences, or a binding script when querying; nothing when setting a binding\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of an element configuration option\&. The available options depend on the concrete
element type\&.
Current value of option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT cget\fP \fIelemName option\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
elemName
Name of the element\&.
.TP
option
Element configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
See \fILINEELEMENT\fP for line-element options, \fISTRIPELEMENT\fP for strip-element options, and \fIBARELEMENT\fP for
bar-element options\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of an element configuration option\&. The available options depend on the concrete
element type\&.
Current value of option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT closest\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Finds the visible element nearest to the specified window coordinate\&. On success, stores information in the
Tcl array varName using the keys name, index, x, y, and dist\&. Searches all visible elements
unless one or more element names are supplied after the options\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT closest\fP \fIx y varName ?option value \&.\&.\&.? ?--? ?elemName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
x
X coordinate in window coordinates\&.
.TP
y
Y coordinate in window coordinates\&.
.TP
varName
Name of the Tcl array in which result information is stored\&.
.TP
-along direction
Measure distance along x, y, or both; the default is both\&.
.TP
-halo distance
Maximum non-negative search distance in screen pixels; the graph -halo value is the default\&.
.TP
-interpolate boolean
For line and strip elements, search along rendered traces instead of only data points\&.
.TP
elemName
Optional element name restricting the search\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
1 if an element is found within the halo, otherwise 0\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies element configuration\&. When querying, one element name is supplied\&. When setting options,
multiple element names may precede the option/value pairs and the same configuration is applied to each named
element\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT configure\fP \fIelemName\fP
.br
\fBELEMENT configure\fP \fIelemName option\fP
.br
\fBELEMENT configure\fP \fIelemName ?elemName \&.\&.\&.? ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
elemName
Name of an element to query or configure\&.
.TP
option
Element configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Available options depend on the concrete element type\&. See \fILINEELEMENT\fP for line-element options,
\fISTRIPELEMENT\fP for strip-element options, and \fIBARELEMENT\fP for bar-element options\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying; nothing when setting options\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT create\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a new graph element\&. GRAPHINST element create uses the widget's default element type:
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT create\fP \fIelemName ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
elemName
Not documented\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.TP
\(bu
graph creates a line element;
.TP
\(bu
barchart creates a bar element;
.TP
\(bu
stripchart creates a strip element\&. GRAPHINST line create always creates a line element and GRAPHINST bar create always creates a bar element, regardless of the widget's default element type\&.
.RS 4n
.TP
elemName
Name of the new element\&.
.TP
option
Element configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Initial value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
See \fILINEELEMENT\fP for line-element options, \fISTRIPELEMENT\fP for strip-element options, and \fIBARELEMENT\fP for
bar-element options\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Name of the newly created element\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT deactivate\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Clears the active state and any active-point selection for one or more elements\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT deactivate\fP \fI?elemName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
elemName
Name of an element to deactivate\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT delete\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Deletes one or more elements and releases their associated resources, bindings, legend entries, vector
references, and display-list entries\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT delete\fP \fI?elemName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
elemName
Name of an element to delete\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT exists\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Tests whether an element with the specified name exists in the graph\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT exists\fP \fIelemName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
elemName
Element name to test\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Boolean value\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT get\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the element currently selected by the graph binding machinery\&. The only supported selector is
current\&. If there is no current element, the result is empty\&.
Current element name or an empty result\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT get\fP \fIname\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
name
Information to retrieve; must currently be current\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the element currently selected by the graph binding machinery\&. The only supported selector is
current\&. If there is no current element, the result is empty\&.
Current element name or an empty result\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT names\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the names of existing graph elements\&. With no patterns, all element names are returned\&. With one or
more Tcl glob patterns, an element is returned if its name matches any pattern\&.
List of matching element names\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT names\fP \fI?pattern \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pattern
Optional Tcl glob pattern used to select element names\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the names of existing graph elements\&. With no patterns, all element names are returned\&. With one or
more Tcl glob patterns, an element is returned if its name matches any pattern\&.
List of matching element names\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT show\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or replaces the element display list\&. With no argument, returns the currently displayed elements in
drawing order\&. With elemList, displays only the listed elements and hides all others\&. The list order
determines drawing order; later elements are drawn above earlier elements\&. Unknown element names are
ignored\&. An empty list hides all elements\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT show\fP \fIelemList\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
elemList
Optional Tcl list of element names to display\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Current element display list\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBELEMENT type\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the concrete class of the named element\&. Current results are LineElement, BarElement, and
StripElement\&.
Element class name\&.
.RS 4n
\fBELEMENT type\fP \fIelemName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
elemName
Name of the element\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the concrete class of the named element\&. Current results are LineElement, BarElement, and
StripElement\&.
Element class name\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBgraph\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a graph widget\&. A graph uses line elements as its default element type\&. The widget supports the
common graph components such as axes, elements, markers, pens, legends, grids, crosshairs, and PostScript
output\&. Configuration options supplied when the widget is created are the same options accepted later by
GRAPHINST configure\&.
.RS 4n
\fBgraph\fP \fIpathName ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pathName
Tk pathname of the graph widget to create\&.
.TP
option
Graph widget configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Value for the configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
graph \&.g -title "Example graph" -width 800 -height 500
pack \&.g -fill both -expand yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The pathname of the newly created graph widget\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIaxis\fP
Performs an operation on the graph's virtual axes\&. Virtual axes can be created, deleted, configured, transformed, bound to events, and mapped to graph margins\&.
.TP
\fIbar\fP
Performs an element operation using bar elements explicitly, regardless of the default element type of the graph widget\&.
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of a graph widget configuration option\&. Current value of option\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies graph widget configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns configuration information for all widget options\&. With one option, returns configuration information for that option\&. With one or more option/value pairs, changes the specified options\&.
.TP
\fIcrosshairs\fP
Performs an operation on the graph crosshairs\&.
.TP
\fIelement\fP
Performs an operation on graph elements using the widget's default element type\&. The default is a line element for graph, a bar element for barchart, and a strip element for stripchart\&.
.TP
\fIextents\fP
Returns the size or geometry of a graph component\&. For plotheight, plotwidth, and the margin items, returns one size in pixels\&. For plotarea and legend, returns a list of four values in the form x y width height\&.
.TP
\fIgrid\fP
Performs an operation on the graph grid\&.
.TP
\fIinside\fP
Tests whether a window coordinate lies inside the graph plotting area\&.
.TP
\fIinvtransform\fP
Converts a point from window coordinates to graph coordinates using the graph's primary X and Y axes\&. winX and winY may be Tcl expressions that evaluate to numeric values\&.
.TP
\fIlegend\fP
Performs an operation on the graph legend\&.
.TP
\fIline\fP
Performs an element operation using line elements explicitly, regardless of the default element type of the graph widget\&.
.TP
\fImarker\fP
Performs an operation on graph markers\&.
.TP
\fIpen\fP
Performs an operation on graph pens\&. Pens define reusable drawing attributes for line and bar elements\&.
.TP
\fIpostscript\fP
Performs a PostScript output operation for the graph\&.
.TP
\fIsnap\fP
Renders a snapshot of the graph\&. By default the snapshot is written to an existing Tk photo image\&. On Windows, snapshots can also be written as Windows Metafile (wmf) or Enhanced Metafile (emf) output\&.
.TP
\fItransform\fP
Converts a point from graph coordinates to window coordinates using the graph's primary X and Y axes\&. x and y may be Tcl expressions that evaluate to numeric values\&.
.TP
\fIx2axis\fP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the secondary X-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this is the top margin\&.
.TP
\fIxaxis\fP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the primary X-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this is the bottom margin\&.
.TP
\fIy2axis\fP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the secondary Y-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this is the right margin\&.
.TP
\fIyaxis\fP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the primary Y-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this is the left margin\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST axis\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the graph's virtual axes\&. Virtual axes can be created, deleted, configured,
transformed, bound to events, and mapped to graph margins\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST axis\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
See \fIAXIS\fP for the complete virtual-axis command and option reference\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected axis operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST bar\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an element operation using bar elements explicitly, regardless of the default element type of the
graph widget\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST bar\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected element operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of a graph widget configuration option\&.
Current value of option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST cget\fP \fIoption\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Graph widget configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
See \fIWidget options\fP for the complete list of supported options and their descriptions\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of a graph widget configuration option\&.
Current value of option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies graph widget configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns configuration information
for all widget options\&. With one option, returns configuration information for that option\&. With one or more
option/value pairs, changes the specified options\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST configure\fP \fIoption\fP
.br
\fBGRAPHINST configure\fP \fI?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Graph widget configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
See \fIWidget options\fP for the complete list of supported options and their descriptions\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying; nothing when setting options\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST crosshairs\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the graph crosshairs\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST crosshairs\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected crosshairs operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST element\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on graph elements using the widget's default element type\&. The default is a line
element for graph, a bar element for barchart, and a strip element for stripchart\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST element\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected element operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST extents\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the size or geometry of a graph component\&.
For plotheight, plotwidth, and the margin items, returns one size in pixels\&. For plotarea and
legend, returns a list of four values in the form x y width height\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST extents\fP \fIitem\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
item
Component whose extents are requested\&. Must be plotheight, plotwidth, plotarea, legend, leftmargin, rightmargin, topmargin, or bottommargin\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the size or geometry of a graph component\&.
For plotheight, plotwidth, and the margin items, returns one size in pixels\&. For plotarea and
legend, returns a list of four values in the form x y width height\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST grid\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the graph grid\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST grid\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected grid operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST inside\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Tests whether a window coordinate lies inside the graph plotting area\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST inside\fP \fIwinX winY\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
winX
X coordinate in window coordinates\&.
.TP
winY
Y coordinate in window coordinates\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Boolean value indicating whether the point is inside the plotting area\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST invtransform\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Converts a point from window coordinates to graph coordinates using the graph's primary X and Y axes\&.
winX and winY may be Tcl expressions that evaluate to numeric values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST invtransform\fP \fIwinX winY\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
winX
X coordinate in window coordinates\&.
.TP
winY
Y coordinate in window coordinates\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A two-element list containing the corresponding graph coordinates x y\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST legend\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the graph legend\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST legend\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected legend operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST line\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an element operation using line elements explicitly, regardless of the default element type of the
graph widget\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST line\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected element operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST marker\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on graph markers\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST marker\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected marker operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST pen\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on graph pens\&. Pens define reusable drawing attributes for line and bar elements\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST pen\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected pen operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST postscript\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs a PostScript output operation for the graph\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST postscript\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected PostScript operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST snap\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Renders a snapshot of the graph\&. By default the snapshot is written to an existing Tk photo image\&. On
Windows, snapshots can also be written as Windows Metafile (wmf) or Enhanced Metafile (emf) output\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST snap\fP \fIname ?-format format? ?-height height? ?-width width?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-format format
Output format\&. photo is available on all platforms\&. wmf and emf are available on Windows\&. The default is photo\&.
.TP
-height height
Height of the rendered snapshot\&. If omitted, the current widget height is used\&.
.TP
-width width
Width of the rendered snapshot\&. If omitted, the current widget width is used\&.
.TP
name
For photo format, name of an existing Tk photo image; for wmf or emf, output filename\&. On Windows, CLIPBOARD writes metafile output to the clipboard instead of a file\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
image create photo graphImage
\&.g snap graphImage

\&.g snap graphImage -width 1200 -height 800
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST transform\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Converts a point from graph coordinates to window coordinates using the graph's primary X and Y axes\&.
x and y may be Tcl expressions that evaluate to numeric values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST transform\fP \fIx y\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
x
X coordinate in graph coordinates\&.
.TP
y
Y coordinate in graph coordinates\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A two-element list containing the corresponding integer window coordinates x y\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST x2axis\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the secondary X-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation
this is the top margin\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST x2axis\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected margin-axis operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST xaxis\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the primary X-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this
is the bottom margin\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST xaxis\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected margin-axis operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST y2axis\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the secondary Y-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation
this is the right margin\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST y2axis\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected margin-axis operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST yaxis\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the primary Y-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this
is the left margin\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST yaxis\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected margin-axis operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRID\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRID\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of one grid configuration option\&. See \fIGrid options\fP\&. Current value of the requested option\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies grid configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns all configuration information\&. With one option, returns information for that option\&. Otherwise args contains option/value pairs to change\&. See \fIGrid options\fP\&.
.TP
\fIoff\fP
Hides the grid\&. This is equivalent to setting -hide yes\&. If the grid is already hidden, the operation has no effect\&.
.TP
\fIon\fP
Displays the grid\&. This is equivalent to setting -hide no\&. If the grid is already displayed, the operation has no effect\&.
.TP
\fItoggle\fP
Toggles grid visibility\&. A visible grid becomes hidden and a hidden grid becomes visible\&. The resulting state is reflected by the -hide configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRID cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of one grid configuration option\&. See \fIGrid options\fP\&.
Current value of the requested option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRID cget\fP \fIoption\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Grid configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of one grid configuration option\&. See \fIGrid options\fP\&.
Current value of the requested option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRID configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies grid configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns all configuration information\&.
With one option, returns information for that option\&. Otherwise args contains option/value pairs to change\&.
See \fIGrid options\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRID configure\fP \fI?option? ?value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Grid configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying, otherwise nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRID off\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Hides the grid\&. This is equivalent to setting -hide yes\&. If the grid is already hidden, the operation has
no effect\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRID off\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRID on\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Displays the grid\&. This is equivalent to setting -hide no\&. If the grid is already displayed, the operation
has no effect\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRID on\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRID toggle\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Toggles grid visibility\&. A visible grid becomes hidden and a hidden grid becomes visible\&. The resulting state
is reflected by the -hide configuration option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRID toggle\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBLEGEND\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBLEGEND\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIactivate\fP
Marks matching legend entries active\&. Each pattern is matched against element names\&. After applying all patterns, the command returns the names of all legend entries that are currently active\&. A pattern that matches no element has no effect\&.
.TP
\fIbind\fP
Queries or changes event bindings for legend entries associated with tagName\&. Element names are implicit binding tags\&. With no sequence, returns the bound event sequences\&. With a sequence but no command, returns the current binding\&. With a sequence and command, creates or replaces the binding; a command beginning with + appends to an existing binding\&.
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of one legend configuration option\&. See \fILegend options\fP\&. Current value of the requested option\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies legend configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns all configuration information\&. With one option, returns information for that option\&. Otherwise args contains option/value pairs to change\&. See \fILegend options\fP\&.
.TP
\fIdeactivate\fP
Marks matching legend entries inactive\&. Each pattern is matched against element names\&. Inactive entries are drawn using the normal legend appearance\&. A pattern that matches no element has no effect\&.
.TP
\fIget\fP
Returns the element name represented by the legend entry at position\&. position may be current, meaning the legend entry currently under the pointer, or an explicit screen coordinate of the form @x,y\&. If no legend entry is present at the requested position, an empty string is returned\&. Name of the corresponding graph element, or an empty string if no entry is found\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBLEGEND activate\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Marks matching legend entries active\&. Each pattern is matched against element names\&. After applying all
patterns, the command returns the names of all legend entries that are currently active\&. A pattern that
matches no element has no effect\&.
.RS 4n
\fBLEGEND activate\fP \fIpattern ?pattern \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pattern
Glob pattern selecting element names whose legend entries are activated\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
List of all currently active legend entries\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBLEGEND bind\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes event bindings for legend entries associated with tagName\&. Element names are implicit
binding tags\&. With no sequence, returns the bound event sequences\&. With a sequence but no command, returns the
current binding\&. With a sequence and command, creates or replaces the binding; a command beginning with +
appends to an existing binding\&.
.RS 4n
\fBLEGEND bind\fP \fItagName ?sequence? ?command?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
tagName
Legend entry binding tag\&.
.TP
command
Optional Tcl command associated with the event sequence\&.
.TP
sequence
Optional event sequence\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Binding information when querying, otherwise nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBLEGEND cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of one legend configuration option\&. See \fILegend options\fP\&.
Current value of the requested option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBLEGEND cget\fP \fIoption\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Legend configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of one legend configuration option\&. See \fILegend options\fP\&.
Current value of the requested option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBLEGEND configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies legend configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns all configuration information\&.
With one option, returns information for that option\&. Otherwise args contains option/value pairs to change\&.
See \fILegend options\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBLEGEND configure\fP \fI?option? ?value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Legend configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying, otherwise nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBLEGEND deactivate\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Marks matching legend entries inactive\&. Each pattern is matched against element names\&. Inactive entries are
drawn using the normal legend appearance\&. A pattern that matches no element has no effect\&.
.RS 4n
\fBLEGEND deactivate\fP \fIpattern ?pattern \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pattern
Glob pattern selecting element names whose legend entries are deactivated\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBLEGEND get\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the element name represented by the legend entry at position\&. position may be current, meaning
the legend entry currently under the pointer, or an explicit screen coordinate of the form @x,y\&. If no
legend entry is present at the requested position, an empty string is returned\&.
Name of the corresponding graph element, or an empty string if no entry is found\&.
.RS 4n
\fBLEGEND get\fP \fIcurrent|@x,y\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
position
current or a screen position of the form @x,y\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the element name represented by the legend entry at position\&. position may be current, meaning
the legend entry currently under the pointer, or an explicit screen coordinate of the form @x,y\&. If no
legend entry is present at the requested position, an empty string is returned\&.
Name of the corresponding graph element, or an empty string if no entry is found\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARGINAXIS\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARGINAXIS\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIbind\fP
Queries or modifies event bindings for the first axis assigned to this margin\&. With no arguments, returns the event sequences currently bound to the axis\&. With a sequence, returns the corresponding binding script\&. With both a sequence and command, creates or replaces the binding\&.
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of a configuration option for the first axis assigned to this margin\&. See \fIAXIS\fP for the complete axis option reference\&. Current value of option\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies configuration options of the first axis assigned to this margin\&. With no arguments, returns configuration information for all axis options\&. With one option, returns configuration information for that option\&. With option/value pairs, changes the specified options\&. See \fIAXIS\fP for the complete axis option reference\&.
.TP
\fIinvtransform\fP
Converts an integer window coordinate to the corresponding graph-coordinate value using the first axis assigned to this margin\&. Axis orientation, descending direction, and logarithmic mapping are taken into account\&.
.TP
\fIlimits\fP
Returns the effective displayed limits of the first axis assigned to this margin\&. Automatic scaling, explicit limits, loose-range processing, and logarithmic mapping are reflected in the result\&. Logarithmic limits are returned as ordinary positive data values rather than base-10 exponents\&. Two-element list min max\&.
.TP
\fItransform\fP
Converts a graph-coordinate value to an integer window coordinate using the first axis assigned to this margin\&. value may be a Tcl numeric expression and must evaluate to a finite value\&. Values transformed through a logarithmic axis must be positive\&.
.TP
\fIuse\fP
Queries or changes the ordered list of virtual axes assigned to this margin\&. With no argument, returns all currently assigned axis names\&. With axisList, replaces the current assignment with the supplied list\&. The first axis becomes the primary axis addressed by the other MARGINAXIS operations\&. Assigning an axis that is already displayed in another margin moves it to this margin\&. An empty list removes all axes\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARGINAXIS bind\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies event bindings for the first axis assigned to this margin\&. With no arguments, returns
the event sequences currently bound to the axis\&. With a sequence, returns the corresponding binding script\&.
With both a sequence and command, creates or replaces the binding\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARGINAXIS bind\fP \fIsequence\fP
.br
\fBMARGINAXIS bind\fP \fIsequence command\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
command
Tcl script to execute for the binding\&.
.TP
sequence
Tk event sequence\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Event sequences or a binding script when querying; nothing when setting a binding\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARGINAXIS cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of a configuration option for the first axis assigned to this margin\&. See \fIAXIS\fP
for the complete axis option reference\&.
Current value of option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARGINAXIS cget\fP \fIoption\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Axis configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of a configuration option for the first axis assigned to this margin\&. See \fIAXIS\fP
for the complete axis option reference\&.
Current value of option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARGINAXIS configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies configuration options of the first axis assigned to this margin\&. With no arguments,
returns configuration information for all axis options\&. With one option, returns configuration information
for that option\&. With option/value pairs, changes the specified options\&. See \fIAXIS\fP for the complete axis
option reference\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARGINAXIS configure\fP \fIoption\fP
.br
\fBMARGINAXIS configure\fP \fI?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Axis configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying; nothing when setting options\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARGINAXIS invtransform\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Converts an integer window coordinate to the corresponding graph-coordinate value using the first axis
assigned to this margin\&. Axis orientation, descending direction, and logarithmic mapping are taken into
account\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARGINAXIS invtransform\fP \fIvalue\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
value
Integer window coordinate\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Corresponding graph-coordinate value\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARGINAXIS limits\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the effective displayed limits of the first axis assigned to this margin\&. Automatic scaling,
explicit limits, loose-range processing, and logarithmic mapping are reflected in the result\&. Logarithmic
limits are returned as ordinary positive data values rather than base-10 exponents\&.
Two-element list min max\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARGINAXIS limits\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the effective displayed limits of the first axis assigned to this margin\&. Automatic scaling,
explicit limits, loose-range processing, and logarithmic mapping are reflected in the result\&. Logarithmic
limits are returned as ordinary positive data values rather than base-10 exponents\&.
Two-element list min max\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARGINAXIS transform\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Converts a graph-coordinate value to an integer window coordinate using the first axis assigned to this
margin\&. value may be a Tcl numeric expression and must evaluate to a finite value\&. Values transformed
through a logarithmic axis must be positive\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARGINAXIS transform\fP \fIvalue\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
value
Graph-coordinate value or Tcl numeric expression\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Corresponding integer window coordinate\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARGINAXIS use\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes the ordered list of virtual axes assigned to this margin\&. With no argument, returns all
currently assigned axis names\&. With axisList, replaces the current assignment with the supplied list\&.
The first axis becomes the primary axis addressed by the other MARGINAXIS operations\&. Assigning an axis
that is already displayed in another margin moves it to this margin\&. An empty list removes all axes\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARGINAXIS use\fP \fIaxisList\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
axisList
Tcl list containing zero or more virtual axis names\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Ordered list of assigned axis names when querying; nothing when changing the assignment\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIafter\fP
Changes the position of a marker in the marker display list\&. If afterMarker is specified, markerName is moved immediately after it\&. If it is omitted, markerName is moved to the end of the marker display list\&.
.TP
\fIbefore\fP
Changes the position of a marker in the marker display list\&. If beforeMarker is specified, markerName is moved immediately before it\&. If it is omitted, markerName is moved to the beginning of the marker display list\&.
.TP
\fIbind\fP
Queries or changes marker bindings\&. With no arguments, returns the known marker binding tags\&. With only a tag name, returns the event sequences bound to that tag\&. With a tag and sequence, returns the binding for that sequence\&. With a command as well, creates or replaces the binding; a command beginning with + appends to the existing binding\&.
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of one configuration option of a marker\&. The available options depend on the concrete marker type\&. See \fIMarker types and options\fP\&. Current value of the requested option\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies marker configuration options\&. One or more marker names may be supplied before the first option\&. With no option, returns all configuration information for the first marker\&. With one option, returns information for that option for the first marker\&. With option/value pairs, applies the same changes to every listed marker\&. See \fIMarker types and options\fP\&.
.TP
\fIcreate\fP
Creates a new marker of the specified type\&. type may be bitmap, image, line, polygon, text, or window\&. Use -name to specify an explicit marker name; otherwise Rbc generates one automatically\&. Creating a marker with the same name as an existing marker replaces the existing marker\&. See \fIMarker types and options\fP\&.
.TP
\fIdelete\fP
Deletes one or more markers\&. Supplying no marker names has no effect\&.
.TP
\fIexists\fP
Reports whether a marker with the specified name exists\&.
.TP
\fIfind\fP
Finds the first displayed marker relative to a rectangular screen region\&. mode may be enclosed, which requires the marker to be contained by the region, or overlapping, which requires it to intersect the region\&. Hidden markers and markers associated with hidden elements are ignored\&.
.TP
\fIget\fP
Returns the marker currently selected by the graph's binding machinery\&. The supported selector is current\&. If there is no current marker, an empty string is returned\&. Name of the current marker, or an empty string\&.
.TP
\fInames\fP
Returns marker names\&. If patterns are supplied, only marker names matching at least one glob pattern are returned\&. List of marker names\&.
.TP
\fItype\fP
Returns the concrete type of an existing marker\&. Marker type: bitmap, image, line, polygon, text, or window\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER after\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Changes the position of a marker in the marker display list\&. If afterMarker is specified, markerName is
moved immediately after it\&. If it is omitted, markerName is moved to the end of the marker display list\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER after\fP \fImarkerName ?afterMarker?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
markerName
Name of the marker to move\&.
.TP
afterMarker
Optional marker after which markerName is placed\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER before\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Changes the position of a marker in the marker display list\&. If beforeMarker is specified, markerName is
moved immediately before it\&. If it is omitted, markerName is moved to the beginning of the marker display
list\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER before\fP \fImarkerName ?beforeMarker?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
markerName
Name of the marker to move\&.
.TP
beforeMarker
Optional marker before which markerName is placed\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER bind\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes marker bindings\&. With no arguments, returns the known marker binding tags\&. With only a
tag name, returns the event sequences bound to that tag\&. With a tag and sequence, returns the binding for
that sequence\&. With a command as well, creates or replaces the binding; a command beginning with + appends
to the existing binding\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER bind\fP \fI?tagName? ?sequence? ?command?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
command
Optional Tcl command to invoke for the binding\&.
.TP
sequence
Optional event sequence\&.
.TP
tagName
Optional marker binding tag\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Binding tags or binding information when querying, otherwise nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of one configuration option of a marker\&. The available options depend on the
concrete marker type\&. See \fIMarker types and options\fP\&.
Current value of the requested option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER cget\fP \fImarkerName option\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
markerName
Name of the marker\&.
.TP
option
Marker configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of one configuration option of a marker\&. The available options depend on the
concrete marker type\&. See \fIMarker types and options\fP\&.
Current value of the requested option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies marker configuration options\&. One or more marker names may be supplied before the first
option\&. With no option, returns all configuration information for the first marker\&. With one option, returns
information for that option for the first marker\&. With option/value pairs, applies the same changes to every
listed marker\&. See \fIMarker types and options\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER configure\fP \fImarkerName ?markerName \&.\&.\&.? ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
markerName
Name of a marker to query or configure\&.
.TP
option
Marker configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying, otherwise nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER create\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a new marker of the specified type\&. type may be bitmap, image, line, polygon, text, or
window\&. Use -name to specify an explicit marker name; otherwise Rbc generates one automatically\&. Creating
a marker with the same name as an existing marker replaces the existing marker\&. See
\fIMarker types and options\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER create\fP \fItype ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
type
Type of marker to create\&.
.TP
option
Initial marker configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Initial value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Name of the newly created marker\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER delete\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Deletes one or more markers\&. Supplying no marker names has no effect\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER delete\fP \fI?markerName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
markerName
Name of a marker to delete\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER exists\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Reports whether a marker with the specified name exists\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER exists\fP \fImarkerName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
markerName
Marker name to test\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
1 if the marker exists and 0 otherwise\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER find\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Finds the first displayed marker relative to a rectangular screen region\&. mode may be enclosed, which
requires the marker to be contained by the region, or overlapping, which requires it to intersect the
region\&. Hidden markers and markers associated with hidden elements are ignored\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER find\fP \fIenclosed|overlapping x1 y1 x2 y2\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
mode
Search mode, enclosed or overlapping\&.
.TP
x1
First X coordinate of the search rectangle\&.
.TP
y1
First Y coordinate of the search rectangle\&.
.TP
x2
Second X coordinate of the search rectangle\&.
.TP
y2
Second Y coordinate of the search rectangle\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Name of the first matching marker, or an empty string if none matches\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER get\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the marker currently selected by the graph's binding machinery\&. The supported selector is current\&.
If there is no current marker, an empty string is returned\&.
Name of the current marker, or an empty string\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER get\fP \fIcurrent\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
name
Marker selector; currently current\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the marker currently selected by the graph's binding machinery\&. The supported selector is current\&.
If there is no current marker, an empty string is returned\&.
Name of the current marker, or an empty string\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER names\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns marker names\&. If patterns are supplied, only marker names matching at least one glob pattern are
returned\&.
List of marker names\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER names\fP \fI?pattern \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pattern
Optional glob pattern used to select marker names\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns marker names\&. If patterns are supplied, only marker names matching at least one glob pattern are
returned\&.
List of marker names\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBMARKER type\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the concrete type of an existing marker\&.
Marker type: bitmap, image, line, polygon, text, or window\&.
.RS 4n
\fBMARKER type\fP \fImarkerName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
markerName
Name of the marker\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the concrete type of an existing marker\&.
Marker type: bitmap, image, line, polygon, text, or window\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPEN\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPEN\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of a pen configuration option\&. Available options depend on whether the named pen is a line or bar pen\&. See \fIline pen options\fP and \fIbar pen options\fP\&. Current value of option\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies pen configuration options\&. One or more pen names may be supplied before the first option\&. With no option, returns all configuration information for the first pen\&. With one option, returns information for that option for the first pen\&. With option/value pairs, applies the same changes to every listed pen\&. See \fIline pen options\fP and \fIbar pen options\fP\&.
.TP
\fIcreate\fP
Creates a new named pen\&. -type line creates a line pen, -type bar creates a bar pen, and -type strip creates a line pen suitable for strip elements\&. If -type is omitted, the graph widget's default element type determines the pen type\&. See \fIline pen options\fP and \fIbar pen options\fP\&.
.TP
\fIdelete\fP
Deletes one or more named pens\&. If a pen is still referenced by an element, destruction is deferred until its final reference is released\&. The deleted pen name immediately stops being available for new references\&.
.TP
\fInames\fP
Returns names of existing pens\&. With one or more Tcl glob patterns, a pen is returned when its name matches any pattern\&. Pens pending deletion are not returned\&. List of matching pen names\&.
.TP
\fItype\fP
Returns the concrete class of the named pen\&. Line and strip pens return LineElement; bar pens return BarElement\&. This reports the actual pen implementation and is independent of the textual creation-time -type representation\&. LineElement or BarElement\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPEN cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of a pen configuration option\&. Available options depend on whether the named pen is
a line or bar pen\&. See \fIline pen options\fP and \fIbar pen options\fP\&.
Current value of option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPEN cget\fP \fIpenName option\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
penName
Name of the pen\&.
.TP
option
Pen configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of a pen configuration option\&. Available options depend on whether the named pen is
a line or bar pen\&. See \fIline pen options\fP and \fIbar pen options\fP\&.
Current value of option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPEN configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies pen configuration options\&. One or more pen names may be supplied before the first option\&.
With no option, returns all configuration information for the first pen\&. With one option, returns information
for that option for the first pen\&. With option/value pairs, applies the same changes to every listed pen\&. See
\fIline pen options\fP and \fIbar pen options\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPEN configure\fP \fIpenName ?penName \&.\&.\&.? ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
penName
Name of a pen to query or configure\&.
.TP
option
Pen configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying, otherwise nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPEN create\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a new named pen\&. -type line creates a line pen, -type bar creates a bar pen, and -type strip
creates a line pen suitable for strip elements\&. If -type is omitted, the graph widget's default element
type determines the pen type\&. See \fIline pen options\fP and \fIbar pen options\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPEN create\fP \fIpenName ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
penName
Unique name of the new pen\&.
.TP
option
Initial pen configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Initial value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Name of the newly created pen\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPEN delete\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Deletes one or more named pens\&. If a pen is still referenced by an element, destruction is deferred until
its final reference is released\&. The deleted pen name immediately stops being available for new references\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPEN delete\fP \fI?penName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
penName
Name of a pen to delete\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPEN names\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns names of existing pens\&. With one or more Tcl glob patterns, a pen is returned when its name matches
any pattern\&. Pens pending deletion are not returned\&.
List of matching pen names\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPEN names\fP \fI?pattern \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pattern
Optional Tcl glob pattern used to select pen names\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns names of existing pens\&. With one or more Tcl glob patterns, a pen is returned when its name matches
any pattern\&. Pens pending deletion are not returned\&.
List of matching pen names\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPEN type\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the concrete class of the named pen\&. Line and strip pens return LineElement; bar pens return
BarElement\&. This reports the actual pen implementation and is independent of the textual creation-time
-type representation\&.
LineElement or BarElement\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPEN type\fP \fIpenName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
penName
Name of the pen\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the concrete class of the named pen\&. Line and strip pens return LineElement; bar pens return
BarElement\&. This reports the actual pen implementation and is independent of the textual creation-time
-type representation\&.
LineElement or BarElement\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPOSTSCRIPT\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPOSTSCRIPT\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of one PostScript configuration option\&. See \fIPostScript options\fP\&. Current value of the requested option\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies PostScript configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns all configuration information\&. With one option, returns information for that option\&. Otherwise args contains option/value pairs to change\&. See \fIPostScript options\fP\&.
.TP
\fIoutput\fP
Generates an Encapsulated PostScript representation of the graph\&. If fileName is supplied, the output is written to that file; otherwise the generated PostScript is returned as the command result\&. Configuration options may be supplied after the optional filename and remain installed after successful output\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPOSTSCRIPT cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of one PostScript configuration option\&. See
\fIPostScript options\fP\&.
Current value of the requested option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPOSTSCRIPT cget\fP \fIoption\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
PostScript configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of one PostScript configuration option\&. See
\fIPostScript options\fP\&.
Current value of the requested option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPOSTSCRIPT configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies PostScript configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns all configuration
information\&. With one option, returns information for that option\&. Otherwise args contains option/value
pairs to change\&. See \fIPostScript options\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPOSTSCRIPT configure\fP \fI?option? ?value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
PostScript configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying, otherwise nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBPOSTSCRIPT output\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Generates an Encapsulated PostScript representation of the graph\&. If fileName is supplied, the output is
written to that file; otherwise the generated PostScript is returned as the command result\&. Configuration
options may be supplied after the optional filename and remain installed after successful output\&.
.RS 4n
\fBPOSTSCRIPT output\fP \fI?fileName? ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
fileName
Optional name of the file to create\&.
.TP
option
Optional PostScript configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Value for the configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Generated PostScript when no filename is supplied, otherwise nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBspline\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBspline\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fInatural\fP
Computes a natural cubic spline through the source vectors x and y and evaluates it at every X coordinate in splX\&. Evaluation coordinates may appear in any order\&. Coordinates outside the source X range are extrapolated using the cubic polynomial of the first or last spline interval\&.
.TP
\fIquadratic\fP
Computes a shape-preserving quadratic spline through the source vectors x and y and evaluates it at every X coordinate in splX\&. Evaluation coordinates may appear in any order\&. Coordinates outside the source X range are extrapolated using the spline defined by the first or last source interval\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBspline natural\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Computes a natural cubic spline through the source vectors x and y and evaluates it at every X
coordinate in splX\&. Evaluation coordinates may appear in any order\&. Coordinates outside the source
X range are extrapolated using the cubic polynomial of the first or last spline interval\&.
.RS 4n
\fBspline natural\fP \fIx y splX splY\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
x
Vector containing the finite, strictly increasing X coordinates of the source points\&.
.TP
y
Vector containing the finite Y coordinates of the source points; it must have the same length as x\&.
.TP
splX
Vector containing finite X coordinates at which the spline is evaluated; values may be in any order and may lie outside the source X range\&.
.TP
splY
Vector in which the interpolated or extrapolated Y coordinates are stored; it is created or resized as necessary\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBspline quadratic\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Computes a shape-preserving quadratic spline through the source vectors x and y and evaluates it at
every X coordinate in splX\&. Evaluation coordinates may appear in any order\&. Coordinates outside the
source X range are extrapolated using the spline defined by the first or last source interval\&.
.RS 4n
\fBspline quadratic\fP \fIx y splX splY\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
x
Vector containing the finite, strictly increasing X coordinates of the source points\&.
.TP
y
Vector containing the finite Y coordinates of the source points; it must have the same length as x\&.
.TP
splX
Vector containing finite X coordinates at which the spline is evaluated; values may be in any order and may lie outside the source X range\&.
.TP
splY
Vector in which the interpolated or extrapolated Y coordinates are stored; it is created or resized as necessary\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBstripchart\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a stripchart widget\&. A stripchart uses strip elements as its default element type\&. It uses the same
graph widget infrastructure and common components as graph, but elements created through the generic
GRAPHINST element interface are strip elements by default\&. Configuration options supplied when the widget
is created are the same options accepted later by GRAPHINST configure\&.
.RS 4n
\fBstripchart\fP \fIpathName ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pathName
Tk pathname of the stripchart widget to create\&.
.TP
option
Graph widget configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Value for the configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
stripchart \&.s -title "Example stripchart" -width 800 -height 500
pack \&.s -fill both -expand yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The pathname of the newly created stripchart widget\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fI*\fP
Multiplies every value of the vector by a scalar or by the corresponding value of another vector\&. list may name another vector or contain a scalar value\&. If another vector is used, it must have the same number of selected values\&.
.TP
\fI+\fP
Adds a scalar or another vector component-by-component\&.
.TP
\fI-\fP
Subtracts a scalar or another vector component-by-component\&.
.TP
\fI/\fP
Divides every vector value by a scalar or by the corresponding value of another vector\&.
.TP
\fIappend\fP
Appends values to the vector\&. Each item may be another vector or a Tcl list of numeric values\&.
.TP
\fIbinread\fP
Reads native binary numeric values from a Tcl channel into the vector\&.
.TP
\fIclear\fP
Clears cached elements of the Tcl array associated with the vector\&. This does not remove values from the vector\&. It can be used to release memory occupied by cached array-index entries\&.
.TP
\fIdelete\fP
Deletes one or more indices or index ranges from the vector\&. Remaining values are compacted after the deleted values are removed\&.
.TP
\fIdup\fP
Copies this vector into another vector\&. The destination vector is created if necessary\&. If it already exists, its contents are replaced\&.
.TP
\fIexpr\fP
Evaluates expression and stores the result in this vector\&.
.TP
\fIindex\fP
Reads or writes a vector value or range\&.  With no value argument, returns the value or values selected by index\&. With value, assigns that numeric value to every element selected by index\&. The special ++end index may be used when setting a value to append one new element\&.
.TP
\fIlength\fP
Queries or changes the vector length\&. If newSize is supplied, the vector is resized\&. New elements created by enlarging the vector are initialized by the vector resizing machinery\&.
.TP
\fImerge\fP
Interleaves values from two or more vectors and stores the result in this vector\&. All input vectors must contain the same number of selected values\&.
.TP
\fInormalize\fP
Normalizes vector values to the range 0\&.0 through 1\&.0 using: (value - minimum) / (maximum - minimum) With no destination vector, returns the normalized values as a Tcl list\&. If vecName is supplied, that vector is created or resized and the normalized values are stored there\&.
.TP
\fIoffset\fP
Queries or changes the external index offset of the vector\&. The offset changes the numeric indices through which the vector is addressed without moving the underlying data\&.
.TP
\fIpopulate\fP
Creates a more densely sampled vector using linear interpolation\&.  density intermediate values are inserted between every adjacent pair of source values\&. density must be at least 1\&. The destination may be the same vector as the source\&.
.TP
\fIrandom\fP
Fills the vector with pseudo-random values in the range [0\&.0, 1\&.0)\&. The current vector length is preserved\&. Each existing component is replaced by a newly generated pseudo-random value\&. An empty vector remains empty\&.
.TP
\fIrange\fP
Returns an inclusive range of vector values\&. If first is less than or equal to last, values are returned in ascending index order\&. If first is greater than last, values are returned in descending index order\&. first and last are normal vector indices and may use forms such as end\&. This operation does not modify the vector\&. A list containing the selected vector values\&.
.TP
\fIsearch\fP
Searches the vector for values in a numeric range\&. With one numeric argument, returns matches equal to that value\&. With two numeric arguments, returns matches between min and max, inclusive within the implementation's floating-point tolerance\&. By default matching vector indices are returned\&. With -value, matching vector values are returned instead\&.
.TP
\fIseq\fP
Generates an arithmetic sequence\&.
.TP
\fIset\fP
Replaces the contents of the vector\&. list may be a Tcl list of numeric values or the name of another vector\&.
.TP
\fIsort\fP
Sorts this vector\&. Additional vectors may be supplied\&. They must have the same length and are rearranged using the same permutation, allowing associated data to remain aligned\&. -reverse requests descending rather than ascending order\&.
.TP
\fIsplit\fP
Distributes values from this vector among destination vectors in round-robin order\&. The source vector length must be evenly divisible by the number of destination vectors\&. Existing destination contents are preserved and the split values are appended\&.
.TP
\fIvariable\fP
Queries or changes the Tcl array variable associated with the vector\&. Supplying an empty variable name removes the current mapping\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST *\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Multiplies every value of the vector by a scalar or by the corresponding value of another vector\&.
list may name another vector or contain a scalar value\&. If another vector is used, it must have the same
number of selected values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST *\fP \fIlist\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
list
Vector name or scalar value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A list containing the resulting values\&. The vector itself is not modified\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST +\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Adds a scalar or another vector component-by-component\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST +\fP \fIlist\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
list
Vector name or scalar value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A list containing the resulting values\&. The vector itself is not modified\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST -\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Subtracts a scalar or another vector component-by-component\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST -\fP \fIlist\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
list
Vector name or scalar value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A list containing the resulting values\&. The vector itself is not modified\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST /\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Divides every vector value by a scalar or by the corresponding value of another vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST /\fP \fIlist\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
list
Vector name or scalar value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A list containing the resulting values\&. The vector itself is not modified\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST append\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Appends values to the vector\&. Each item may be another vector or a Tcl list of numeric values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST append\fP \fIitem ?item \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
item
Vector name or list of numeric values\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST binread\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Reads native binary numeric values from a Tcl channel into the vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST binread\fP \fIchannel ?numValues? ?-at index? ?-format format? ?-swap?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-at index
Starts storing values at index\&. Existing values are overwritten from that position\&. Without -at, values are appended to the vector\&.
.TP
-format format
Native numeric input representation\&. Formats have the form i#, u#, r#, or f#, where # is the native type size in bytes\&. The default is the native C double representation\&.
.TP
-swap
Reverses the byte order of each input value\&.
.TP
channel
Name of a readable Tcl channel\&.
.TP
numValues
Optional maximum number of values to read\&. A value of zero, or omission of the argument, reads until EOF\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The channel is temporarily placed in binary translation mode and its previous translation setting is
restored before the command returns\&. A read that ends in the middle of a binary value is an error\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Number of complete values read\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST clear\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Clears cached elements of the Tcl array associated with the vector\&. This does not remove values from the
vector\&. It can be used to release memory occupied by cached array-index entries\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST clear\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST delete\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Deletes one or more indices or index ranges from the vector\&. Remaining values are compacted after the
deleted values are removed\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST delete\fP \fIindex ?index \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
index
Vector index or colon-separated index range\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST dup\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Copies this vector into another vector\&. The destination vector is created if necessary\&. If it already
exists, its contents are replaced\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST dup\fP \fIvecName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Destination vector\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST expr\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Evaluates expression and stores the result in this vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST expr\fP \fIexpression\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
expression
Vector expression\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST index\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Reads or writes a vector value or range\&.  With no value argument, returns the value or values selected by
index\&. With value, assigns that numeric value to every element selected by index\&. The special
++end index may be used when setting a value to append one new element\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST index\fP \fIindex\fP
.br
\fBVECINST index\fP \fIindex value\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
index
Vector index or range\&.
.TP
value
Optional numeric value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Selected value(s) when querying, or the assigned value when setting\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST length\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes the vector length\&. If newSize is supplied, the vector is resized\&. New elements
created by enlarging the vector are initialized by the vector resizing machinery\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST length\fP \fInewSize\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
newSize
New non-negative vector length\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Current vector length\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST merge\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Interleaves values from two or more vectors and stores the result in this vector\&. All input vectors must
contain the same number of selected values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST merge\fP \fIvecName ?vecName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Input vector\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
For vectors a={1 2} and b={10 20}, merging a b produces:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
1 10 2 20
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST normalize\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Normalizes vector values to the range 0\&.0 through 1\&.0 using: (value - minimum) / (maximum - minimum)
With no destination vector, returns the normalized values as a Tcl list\&. If vecName is supplied, that
vector is created or resized and the normalized values are stored there\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST normalize\fP \fIvecName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Optional destination vector\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Normalized list when no destination is specified; otherwise nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST offset\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes the external index offset of the vector\&. The offset changes the numeric indices
through which the vector is addressed without moving the underlying data\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST offset\fP \fIoffset\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
offset
New Tcl-sized integer offset\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Current offset\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST populate\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a more densely sampled vector using linear interpolation\&.  density intermediate values are
inserted between every adjacent pair of source values\&. density must be at least 1\&. The destination may
be the same vector as the source\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST populate\fP \fIvecName density\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Destination vector\&.
.TP
density
Number of interpolated values inserted between each pair\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST random\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Fills the vector with pseudo-random values in the range [0\&.0, 1\&.0)\&. The current vector length is
preserved\&. Each existing component is replaced by a newly generated pseudo-random value\&. An empty vector
remains empty\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST random\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
x length 100
x random
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST range\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns an inclusive range of vector values\&. If first is less than or equal to last, values are
returned in ascending index order\&. If first is greater than last, values are returned in descending
index order\&. first and last are normal vector indices and may use forms such as end\&. This
operation does not modify the vector\&.
A list containing the selected vector values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST range\fP \fIfirst last\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
first
First vector index\&.
.TP
last
Last vector index\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
x set {10 20 30 40 50}

x range 1 3
# -> 20\&.0 30\&.0 40\&.0

x range 3 1
# -> 40\&.0 30\&.0 20\&.0
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns an inclusive range of vector values\&. If first is less than or equal to last, values are
returned in ascending index order\&. If first is greater than last, values are returned in descending
index order\&. first and last are normal vector indices and may use forms such as end\&. This
operation does not modify the vector\&.
A list containing the selected vector values\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST search\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Searches the vector for values in a numeric range\&. With one numeric argument, returns matches equal to
that value\&. With two numeric arguments, returns matches between min and max, inclusive within the
implementation's floating-point tolerance\&. By default matching vector indices are returned\&. With -value,
matching vector values are returned instead\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST search\fP \fIvalue\fP
.br
\fBVECINST search\fP \fIvalue1 value2\fP
.br
\fBVECINST search\fP \fI-value value\fP
.br
\fBVECINST search\fP \fI-value value1 value2\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-value
Return values instead of indices\&.
.TP
max
Optional upper bound\&.
.TP
min
Lower bound, or the exact value when max is omitted\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
List of matching indices or values\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST seq\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Generates an arithmetic sequence\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST seq\fP \fIstart end ?step?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
end
Numeric end value or literal end\&.
.TP
start
First value\&.
.TP
step
Optional increment\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
With a numeric end, the vector is resized to hold the generated sequence:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
x seq 0 10 0\&.5
.fi
.RE
.PP
If the second argument is the literal end, the vector keeps its current length and is filled starting at
start using step:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
x length 100
x seq 0 end 0\&.1
.fi
.RE
.PP
step defaults to 1\&.0 and must be non-zero\&. Sequence bounds and step must be finite\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST set\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Replaces the contents of the vector\&. list may be a Tcl list of numeric values or the name of another
vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST set\fP \fIlist\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
list
Numeric list or vector name\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST sort\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Sorts this vector\&. Additional vectors may be supplied\&. They must have the same length and are
rearranged using the same permutation, allowing associated data to remain aligned\&. -reverse requests
descending rather than ascending order\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST sort\fP \fI?-reverse? ?vecName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-reverse
Sort in descending order\&.
.TP
vecName
Optional associated vector to reorder\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST split\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Distributes values from this vector among destination vectors in round-robin order\&. The source vector
length must be evenly divisible by the number of destination vectors\&. Existing destination contents are
preserved and the split values are appended\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST split\fP \fI?vecName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Destination vector\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
For example, splitting:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
{1 10 2 20 3 30}
.fi
.RE
.PP
into vectors x and y appends {1 2 3} to x and {10 20 30} to y\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST variable\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes the Tcl array variable associated with the vector\&. Supplying an empty variable name
removes the current mapping\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST variable\fP \fIvarName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
varName
Optional Tcl array variable name\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Name of the currently mapped variable, or an empty result if no variable is mapped\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBvector\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBvector\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIcreate\fP
Creates one or more vectors\&.
.TP
\fIdestroy\fP
Destroys one or more vectors\&. Destroying a vector removes its associated vector instance command and Tcl array variable and notifies clients that use the vector\&.
.TP
\fIexpr\fP
Evaluates a vector expression\&. expression may contain scalars, vectors, vector ranges, arithmetic operators, and the vector mathematical functions described in \fIVector expressions\fP\&. Unlike the instance \fIVECINST::expr\fP operation, this command does not store the result in an existing vector\&.
.TP
\fInames\fP
Returns the names of existing vectors\&. If pattern is supplied, only vector names matching the Tcl glob pattern are returned\&. A list of vector names\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBvector create\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates one or more vectors\&.
.RS 4n
\fBvector create\fP \fIvecName ?vecName \&.\&.\&.? ?switch value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-command commandName
Sets the Tcl command associated with the vector\&. This option can only be used when creating one vector\&.
.TP
-flush boolean
If true, cached entries in the associated Tcl array are flushed whenever the vector is modified\&.
.TP
-length size
Sets the initial length when no size is included in the vector specification\&. size must be zero or greater\&.
.TP
-variable varName
Sets the Tcl array variable associated with the vector\&. This option can only be used when creating one vector\&.
.TP
-watchunset boolean
If true, unsetting the complete associated array variable destroys the vector\&.
.TP
vecName
Vector name or vector specification\&. A specification may have the form name, name(size), or name(first:last)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
::rbc::vector create x
::rbc::vector create y(100)
::rbc::vector create z(-10:10)
set v [::rbc::vector create #auto]
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The name of the last vector created\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBvector destroy\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Destroys one or more vectors\&. Destroying a vector removes its associated vector instance command and Tcl
array variable and notifies clients that use the vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBvector destroy\fP \fIvecName ?vecName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Name of a vector to destroy\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBvector expr\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Evaluates a vector expression\&. expression may contain scalars, vectors, vector ranges, arithmetic
operators, and the vector mathematical functions described in \fIVector expressions\fP\&. Unlike the instance
\fIVECINST::expr\fP operation, this command does not store the result in an existing vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBvector expr\fP \fIexpression\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
expression
Vector expression to evaluate\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The scalar value or list of vector values produced by the expression\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBvector names\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the names of existing vectors\&. If pattern is supplied, only vector names matching the Tcl glob
pattern are returned\&.
A list of vector names\&.
.RS 4n
\fBvector names\fP \fIpattern\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pattern
Optional Tcl glob pattern\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the names of existing vectors\&. If pattern is supplied, only vector names matching the Tcl glob
pattern are returned\&.
A list of vector names\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIchanges\fP
Realizes a Tk window if necessary\&. For a toplevel window, also requests backing-store and save-under attributes from the underlying window system\&.
.TP
\fIcolormap\fP
Returns information about color cells in the colormap associated with a Tk window\&. This operation is available only on non-Windows builds and examines up to 256 colormap entries\&. A flat list of color and pixel-value pairs in the form color pixel color pixel \&.\&.\&.\&.
.TP
\fIconvolve\fP
Applies a two-dimensional convolution kernel to a Tk photo image\&. filter must be a non-empty square list of finite numeric values\&. The output has the same dimensions as the source photo\&. The source and destination may be the same image\&.
.TP
\fIimage\fP
Dispatches grouped image-processing operations\&. See \fIWINOPIMAGE\fP for the available subcommands\&.
.TP
\fIlower\fP
Lowers one or more Tk windows to the bottom of their stacking order\&. Supplying no window names has no effect\&.
.TP
\fImap\fP
Maps one or more windows through the underlying window system\&. Tk path names are the portable form of window identifier\&. Supplying no windows has no effect\&.
.TP
\fImove\fP
Moves a Tk window to the specified coordinates using Tk's window-moving operation\&. x and y are parsed as Tk screen distances\&.
.TP
\fIquantize\fP
Reduces the number of colors in a Tk photo image\&. nColors must be between 1 and 256\&. If omitted it defaults to 1\&. The destination is resized to the source dimensions when necessary\&.
.TP
\fIquery\fP
Returns the current pointer location in root-screen coordinates\&. Pointer position in the form @x,y, or an empty result if the underlying pointer query fails\&.
.TP
\fIraise\fP
Raises one or more Tk windows to the top of their stacking order\&. Supplying no window names has no effect\&.
.TP
\fIreadjpeg\fP
Reads a JPEG file into an existing Tk photo image\&. The operation returns an error if JPEG support was not compiled into Rbc\&.
.TP
\fIresample\fP
Copies or resamples a source photo into a destination photo\&. The destination's existing dimensions determine the requested output size\&. One filter is used in both directions; two filters independently select horizontal and vertical filtering\&. With no filter, none is used\&. See \fIImage resampling\fP\&.
.TP
\fIsnap\fP
Captures a window into an existing Tk photo image\&. Optional positive width and height values request a resampled output size\&. If either dimension is omitted, the corresponding window dimension is used\&.
.TP
\fIsubsample\fP
Extracts a rectangular source region and copies or resamples it into a destination photo\&. x and y must be non-negative, while width and height must be positive\&. The region must lie completely inside the source image\&. The default filter is box\&. See \fISubsampling\fP\&.
.TP
\fIunmap\fP
Unmaps one or more windows through the underlying window system\&. Tk path names are the portable form of window identifier\&. Supplying no windows has no effect\&.
.TP
\fIwarpto\fP
Queries or changes the pointer position\&. With no argument, returns the current root-screen position\&. An @x,y argument moves the pointer to those root-screen coordinates\&. A Tk window pathname moves the pointer to the center of that window, which must be mapped\&. The resulting pointer position is returned\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop changes\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Realizes a Tk window if necessary\&. For a toplevel window, also requests backing-store and save-under
attributes from the underlying window system\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop changes\fP \fIwindow\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
window
Path name of the Tk window\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop colormap\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns information about color cells in the colormap associated with a Tk window\&. This operation is
available only on non-Windows builds and examines up to 256 colormap entries\&.
A flat list of color and pixel-value pairs in the form color pixel color pixel \&.\&.\&.\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop colormap\fP \fIwindow\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
window
Path name of the Tk window\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns information about color cells in the colormap associated with a Tk window\&. This operation is
available only on non-Windows builds and examines up to 256 colormap entries\&.
A flat list of color and pixel-value pairs in the form color pixel color pixel \&.\&.\&.\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop convolve\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Applies a two-dimensional convolution kernel to a Tk photo image\&. filter must be a non-empty square list
of finite numeric values\&. The output has the same dimensions as the source photo\&. The source and destination
may be the same image\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop convolve\fP \fIsrcPhoto destPhoto filter\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
srcPhoto
Source Tk photo image\&.
.TP
destPhoto
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.TP
filter
Square list of convolution-kernel values\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop image\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Dispatches grouped image-processing operations\&. See \fIWINOPIMAGE\fP for the available subcommands\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop image\fP \fIsubcommand ?arg \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
subcommand
Image operation\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected image operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop lower\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Lowers one or more Tk windows to the bottom of their stacking order\&. Supplying no window names has no
effect\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop lower\fP \fI?window \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
window
Path name of a Tk window\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop map\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Maps one or more windows through the underlying window system\&. Tk path names are the portable form of window
identifier\&. Supplying no windows has no effect\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop map\fP \fI?window \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
window
Window to map\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop move\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Moves a Tk window to the specified coordinates using Tk's window-moving operation\&. x and y are parsed as
Tk screen distances\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop move\fP \fIwindow x y\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
window
Path name of the Tk window\&.
.TP
x
New horizontal position\&.
.TP
y
New vertical position\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop quantize\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Reduces the number of colors in a Tk photo image\&. nColors must be between 1 and 256\&. If omitted it
defaults to 1\&. The destination is resized to the source dimensions when necessary\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop quantize\fP \fIsrcPhoto destPhoto ?nColors?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
srcPhoto
Source Tk photo image\&.
.TP
destPhoto
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.TP
nColors
Optional maximum number of colors\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop query\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current pointer location in root-screen coordinates\&.
Pointer position in the form @x,y, or an empty result if the underlying pointer query fails\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop query\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current pointer location in root-screen coordinates\&.
Pointer position in the form @x,y, or an empty result if the underlying pointer query fails\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop raise\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Raises one or more Tk windows to the top of their stacking order\&. Supplying no window names has no effect\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop raise\fP \fI?window \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
window
Path name of a Tk window\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop readjpeg\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Reads a JPEG file into an existing Tk photo image\&. The operation returns an error if JPEG support was not
compiled into Rbc\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop readjpeg\fP \fIfileName photoName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
fileName
JPEG file to read\&.
.TP
photoName
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop resample\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Copies or resamples a source photo into a destination photo\&. The destination's existing dimensions determine
the requested output size\&. One filter is used in both directions; two filters independently select horizontal
and vertical filtering\&. With no filter, none is used\&. See \fIImage resampling\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop resample\fP \fIsrcPhoto destPhoto ?horzFilter? ?vertFilter?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
srcPhoto
Source Tk photo image\&.
.TP
destPhoto
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.TP
horzFilter
Optional horizontal resampling filter\&.
.TP
vertFilter
Optional vertical resampling filter\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop snap\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Captures a window into an existing Tk photo image\&. Optional positive width and height values request a
resampled output size\&. If either dimension is omitted, the corresponding window dimension is used\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop snap\fP \fIwindow photoName ?width? ?height?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
window
Window to capture\&.
.TP
photoName
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.TP
height
Optional positive output height\&.
.TP
width
Optional positive output width\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop subsample\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Extracts a rectangular source region and copies or resamples it into a destination photo\&. x and y must
be non-negative, while width and height must be positive\&. The region must lie completely inside the
source image\&. The default filter is box\&. See \fISubsampling\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop subsample\fP \fIsrcPhoto destPhoto x y width height ?horzFilter? ?vertFilter?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
srcPhoto
Source Tk photo image\&.
.TP
destPhoto
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.TP
x
Horizontal source-region origin\&.
.TP
y
Vertical source-region origin\&.
.TP
width
Source-region width\&.
.TP
height
Source-region height\&.
.TP
horzFilter
Optional horizontal resampling filter\&.
.TP
vertFilter
Optional vertical resampling filter\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop unmap\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Unmaps one or more windows through the underlying window system\&. Tk path names are the portable form of
window identifier\&. Supplying no windows has no effect\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop unmap\fP \fI?window \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
window
Window to unmap\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwinop warpto\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes the pointer position\&. With no argument, returns the current root-screen position\&. An
@x,y argument moves the pointer to those root-screen coordinates\&. A Tk window pathname moves the pointer to
the center of that window, which must be mapped\&. The resulting pointer position is returned\&.
.RS 4n
\fBwinop warpto\fP \fI?window?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
window
Optional mapped Tk window pathname or root-screen position of the form @x,y\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Current pointer position in the form @x,y\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIconvolve\fP
Applies a two-dimensional convolution kernel to a Tk photo image\&. This is the grouped form of \fI::rbc::winop::convolve\fP\&.
.TP
\fIgradient\fP
Generates a gradient directly into an existing Tk photo image\&. left and right are Tk colors\&. type may be linear, radial, rectangular, or blank\&. The current dimensions of photo determine the generated image size\&.
.TP
\fIreadjpeg\fP
Reads a JPEG file into an existing Tk photo image\&. This is the grouped form of \fI::rbc::winop::readjpeg\fP\&.
.TP
\fIresample\fP
Copies or resamples a source photo into a destination photo\&. This is the grouped form of \fI::rbc::winop::resample\fP\&.
.TP
\fIrotate\fP
Rotates a Tk photo image by a finite angle expressed in degrees\&. angle may be a Tcl numeric expression\&. The destination photo is resized to contain the rotated result\&.
.TP
\fIsnap\fP
Captures a window into an existing Tk photo image\&. This is the grouped form of \fI::rbc::winop::snap\fP\&.
.TP
\fIsubsample\fP
Extracts and optionally resamples a rectangular region of a source photo\&. This is the grouped form of \fI::rbc::winop::subsample\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE convolve\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Applies a two-dimensional convolution kernel to a Tk photo image\&. This is the grouped form of
\fI::rbc::winop::convolve\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE convolve\fP \fIsrcPhoto destPhoto filter\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
srcPhoto
Source Tk photo image\&.
.TP
destPhoto
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.TP
filter
Square list of convolution-kernel values\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE gradient\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Generates a gradient directly into an existing Tk photo image\&. left and right are Tk colors\&. type may
be linear, radial, rectangular, or blank\&. The current dimensions of photo determine the generated
image size\&.
.RS 4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE gradient\fP \fIphoto left right type\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
photo
Tk photo image to modify\&.
.TP
left
First gradient color\&.
.TP
right
Second gradient color\&.
.TP
type
Gradient type\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE readjpeg\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Reads a JPEG file into an existing Tk photo image\&. This is the grouped form of
\fI::rbc::winop::readjpeg\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE readjpeg\fP \fIfileName photoName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
fileName
JPEG file to read\&.
.TP
photoName
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE resample\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Copies or resamples a source photo into a destination photo\&. This is the grouped form of
\fI::rbc::winop::resample\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE resample\fP \fIsrcPhoto destPhoto ?horzFilter? ?vertFilter?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
srcPhoto
Source Tk photo image\&.
.TP
destPhoto
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.TP
horzFilter
Optional horizontal resampling filter\&.
.TP
vertFilter
Optional vertical resampling filter\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE rotate\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Rotates a Tk photo image by a finite angle expressed in degrees\&. angle may be a Tcl numeric expression\&. The
destination photo is resized to contain the rotated result\&.
.RS 4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE rotate\fP \fIsrcPhoto destPhoto angle\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
srcPhoto
Source Tk photo image\&.
.TP
destPhoto
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.TP
angle
Rotation angle in degrees\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE snap\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Captures a window into an existing Tk photo image\&. This is the grouped form of \fI::rbc::winop::snap\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE snap\fP \fIwindow photoName ?width? ?height?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
window
Window to capture\&.
.TP
photoName
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.TP
height
Optional positive output height\&.
.TP
width
Optional positive output width\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE subsample\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Extracts and optionally resamples a rectangular region of a source photo\&. This is the grouped form of
\fI::rbc::winop::subsample\fP\&.
.RS 4n
\fBWINOPIMAGE subsample\fP \fIsrcPhoto destPhoto x y width height ?horzFilter? ?vertFilter?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
srcPhoto
Source Tk photo image\&.
.TP
destPhoto
Destination Tk photo image\&.
.TP
x
Horizontal source-region origin\&.
.TP
y
Vertical source-region origin\&.
.TP
width
Source-region width\&.
.TP
height
Source-region height\&.
.TP
horzFilter
Optional horizontal resampling filter\&.
.TP
vertFilter
Optional vertical resampling filter\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.

